margins (76.5% [Aggregator]), EBITDA margins ~41-46% [Estimate] \u2014 an incumbent profit pool, not a cyclical trough/peak story.\n3. Value driver is operational execution (integration, deleveraging, FMC), not technology optionality or a binary event.\nArchetype transition: No. ROIC is near WACC; leverage 2.73x [Actual]; it is not yet a Quality Compounder (needs ROIC>WACC for 3 consecutive years and lower leverage).\n\nStep 0-2. Valuation Engine: ENGINE 1 (Traditional DCF + Multiples), complemented by an equity-FCF (eFCF) cross-check because the company itself guides eFCF.\nWhy Engine 1 is appropriate: telecoms have forecastable cash flows, high D&A, and debt-heavy capital structures \u2014 FCFF/(WACC\u2212g) plus EV bridge is the natural tool.\nEngine error check: An options-based Engine 4 would overvalue the ordinary telco; a cyclical Engine 2 would misfire because TIGO's earnings volatility is driven by FX and M&A, not capacity/price cycles.\n\nStep 0-3. Data Reliability (Data Tier): Tier 1 for FY2016-25 financials [Filed]; Tier 2 for price/positioning [Aggregator]; Tier 3 for Q2'26 operating KPIs [Company/Secondary]. Critical gaps: no TTM, no segment revenue, no quarterly history held, no analyst Q&A, no live macro snapshot, and inconsistently-defined debt figures [Unconfirmed]. Model Confidence: MODERATE-HIGH.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\nStep 1-1. Macro variables (as-of 2026-08; snapshot not retrievable \u2014 [Assumption]):\n\u2022 10-yr UST: ~4.2% [Assumption]; real yield ~2.0% [Assumption]\n\u2022 DXY: ~97 [Assumption]; direction neutral-to-soft\n\u2022 Fed: on hold, easing bias [Assumption]\n\u2022 Global liquidity: stable; EM credit spreads tight but widening off lows [Assumption]\n\u2022 LatAm cycle: mixed \u2014 Colombia fiscal tightening, Guatemala/Honduras stable, Paraguay strong, Chile competitive stress [Estimate]\n\nStep 1-2. Regime distribution [Assumption]: Goldilocks 30% / Reflation 35% / Stagflation 20% / Recession 15% (sums to 100%).\n\nStep 1-3. Macro sensitivity (5 axes, \u22123 to +3):\n\u2022 Interest rates: \u22122 (large USD debt; see Section 6B, leases $2.587B FY25 [Filed])\n\u2022 Dollar: \u22122 (local-currency EBITDA vs USD debt and capex)\n\u2022 Liquidity: \u22121 (EM flow-dependent; 49.7% institutional ownership)\n\u2022 Economic cycle: \u22121 (telecoms defensive, but prepaid mix is cyclical)\n\u2022 Commodities: 0 (no direct exposure)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: Regime-weighted impact \u2248 \u22120.7 \u2192 HEADWIND. Conviction \u22120.5; implement scale-in entry rather than a full initiation.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates:\n\u2022 5-year survival of a leveraged stable-incumbent telecom: ~85-90% [Assumption/industry base rate]\n\u2022 Company already FCF-positive; probability of remaining FCF-positive over 5 years: ~70% [Assumption]\n\u2022 Large M&A (Coltel, Telef\u00f3nica Ecuador, Chile) generating ROIC>WACC: ~25-35%; for in-market tuck-ins, ~50-60% [Assumption]\n\u2022 Platform/FMC transition materially lifting ROIC: ~20-30% [Assumption]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate: My central case embeds ~50% odds of integration success \u2014 consistent with the in-market M&A base rate, NOT above it. No abuse of the base-rate check.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: Success probability (~50-55%) < 1.5x base rate (~75%). No red flag.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\nStep 2-1. Business understanding: TIGO is a Latin American telecom operator (mobile, home broadband, pay-TV, B2B) holding #1/#2 positions in Colombia, Guatemala, Honduras, Paraguay, Bolivia, Panama, with new Ecuador and Chile operations. It is integrating recently-acquired assets and migrating prepaid users to higher-value postpaid/FMC bundles to convert operating leverage into free cash flow. Understandability: High.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0-3 each):\n\u2022 Intangible assets (brand, spectrum): 3\n\u2022 Switching costs (postpaid, FMC bundles): 2