2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business understanding (3 sentences):\n  Millicom is a Latin American mobile/fixed telecom operator (Tigo brand) serving ~60M\n  customers across Colombia, Guatemala, Paraguay, Bolivia, Panama, Uruguay, Ecuador,\n  Chile and Costa Rica. It sells prepaid/postpaid mobile, home broadband and pay-TV,\n  and B2B digital services (cloud, cybersecurity). The 2025\u201326 acquisitions (Telef\u00f3nica\n  Ecuador, Telef\u00f3nica Uruguay, Coltel full control, and Telef\u00f3nica Chile) transformed it\n  into a larger convergent operator in a consolidation-driven region.\n  Understandability: Medium (multi-country, multi-currency, FX-translation complexity).\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0\u20133 each):\n  Intangible assets (spectrum, Tigo brand):         2\n  Switching costs (postpaid contracts, shared bundles): 2\n  Network effects:                                  1 (B2B regional network)\n  Cost advantage:                                   2 (convergence-owned infrastructure)\n  Economies of scale:                               2 (Coltel adds density in Colombia)\n  Regulatory barriers:                              2 (spectrum, concessions, gov stake)\n  Data moat:                                        1 (B2B digital, CRM data)\n  TOTAL: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE moat.\n  Moat direction: Colombia WIDENING (Coltel integration, Q2 org +11% [Actual]);\n  Ecuador/Chile STABILIZING; Guatemala STABLE-WIDENING (+5.9% strongest in 10yrs [Actual]).\n  Narrowing signals: Chile aggressive pricing/churn [Actual \u2014 call]; sector commoditization [Estimate].\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: STALWART transitioning to FAST GROWER.\n  Core checkpoint (Stalwart): PEG 0.54 [Tier 2] supports growth-at-reasonable-price.\n  Growth checkpoint: 5-yr revenue CAGR 8.9% [Actual]; adjusted EBITDA +46.8%