xpense $1B/yr vs EBIT $1.8B. Trigger: FY26 leverage\n     not below 2.6x; or interest coverage <1.5x.\n  R4 Legal/governance: $296M claims vs $47M provision [Actual]; DPA history ($118M [Actual]).\n     Trigger: new DOJ/SEC action or provisions > $150M.\n  R5 Ecuador H2 margin contraction + Chile churn [Actual call]; trigger: group adj. EBITDA\n     margin <43% for two quarters.\nCatalysts:\n  C1 FY26 eFCF \u2265$1.1B delivered (evidence in Q3/Q4 prints);\n  C2 Leverage below 2.5x at YE26 with dividend maintained;\n  C3 Colombia 5G monetisation (1,000 new sites, ~12% capex [call]) starts showing ARPU lift;\n  C4 B2B digital (cloud/cybersecurity) >15% growth;\n  C5 Downgrade of Guatemala/Bolivia political risk premium (macro).\n\nPre-mortem (Layer 7.4) \u2014 five-year failure story:\n  1. \"The Colombia consolidation never produced the synergies; the price war with Movistar\n     and Claro kept margins at 41%; leverage stuck at 3.2x with $1.1B interest; the stock\n     trades at $48.\" Early warning: Colombia service-rev growth <5% by Q4-2026, churn rising.\n  2. \"Bolivia devalued 40% after a political shock; the USD-debt burden overwhelmed equity;\n     dividend cut to $1.00.\" Early warning: parallel BOP spread widening; bond spreads >600bp.\n  3. \"A second corruption finding in Guatemala triggered a $250M fine and a governance\n     overhaul; institutional investors exited.\" Early warning: new DOJ/SEC subpoena,\n     insider sells.\n  Each pre-mortem scenario has a defined watch item above.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nCommon bear triggers with other portfolio assets: USD strength (DXY rally) hits all\n  LatAm FX; higher UST yields hit all high-leverage names; EM risk-off hits TIGO with\n  beta to EM credit spreads. Tail correlation in stress: EM telecoms historically\n  ~0.6\u20130.8 in 2020-style crashes [Estimate].\nCVaR (80% confidence, one-year): if the bear case (\u221225%) materialises, portfolio impact\n  at 4.5% weight = \u22121.1% total portfolio. Acceptable. [Arithmetic]\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nThesis KPIs (quarterly):\n  T1 Colombia organic service revenue growth \u2265 8% (Q2-26: +11% [Actual])\n  T2 Group adjusted EBITDA margin \u2265 45% (Q2-26: 46.3% [Actual])\n  T3 FY26 eFCF \u2265 $0.95B at Q3 run-rate (guidance ~$1.1B [Actual])\n  T4 Capex \u2264 12.5% of revenue (guidance ~12% [Actual])\n  T5 Net-debt/EBITDA < 2.6x by YE26 (H1-26: 2.73x [Actual])\n  T6 FMC penetration \u2265 40%, trending 45% by YE27 [Assumption]\n  T7 Ecuador EBITDA margin: watch \u2265 30% after H2 brand-launch dip [Assumption]\nRe-evaluation triggers: any T1\u2013T5 breach for two consecutive quarters; BOB parallel\n  spread >5%; new regulatory/legal action; CEO/CFO departure; M&A beyond guided scope.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nShort thesis (using same data):\n  1. The 52% H1 revenue growth is a consolidation illusion; organic service revenue is\n     +5.4% while the stock has nearly doubled from $44 to $94.\n  2. Forward P/E on FY26 normalised EPS is ~30x (FY26 NP ~$500M [Estimate] after\n     interest $1B and D&A $1.95B) \u2014 the market is paying growth multiples for a 5%\n     organic grower.\n  3. Leverage 2.73x on a $8.1B debt stack, $1.1B due within a year, in a 4.71% rate\n     environment; eFCF $1.1B covers the dividend $0.75B but leaves only $0.35B for\n     deleveraging \u2014 \"guided deleveraging\" is largely dividend-financed optimism.\n  4. Bolivia new FX regime is an uncontrolled variable; Guatemala has $296M legal\n     claims with only $47M provisioned after a $118M bribery DPA.\n  5. Record margins (Paraguay 56.9%) are mean-reverting; Colombia/Chile competition\n     is intensifying; Ecuador brand-launch costs hit H2.\n  6. The turnaround story has been \"one quarter away\" before: Q1-26 EPS $0.65 vs\n     expected $0.85 (miss [Actual \u2014 web brief]).\n  7. 6.3x EV/EBITDA is not cheap for LatAm telecoms \u2014 Am\u00e9rica M\u00f3vil trades at ~5.5x\n     with a stronger balance sheet.\n\nLogic combat (Long vs Short):\n  Long logic strength: 13/20 \u2014 grounded in actual eFCF, guided FCF step-up, real MoS.\n  Short logic strength: 11/20 \u2014 valid on leverage/FX, but misses that the market has\n  ALREADY discounted much of it (6.3x multiple, analyst target only +3%).\n  Gap = 2 \u2192 conviction \u22120.5 step (already applied).\n\nAdversarial data: Hindenburg/activist reports: NONE found [Unconfirmed \u2014 no campaign].\n  DPA history is the effective adversarial evidence [Actual \u2014 DOJ 2024].\nKilled arguments (with resurrection conditions):\n  - \"eFCF guidance is just timing (H1 +56% partly due to expense timing [call])\" \u2014\n    KILLED for 2026 because guidance was RAISED from \u2265$900M to ~$1.1B. RESURRECT if\n    Q3-26 eFCF < $250M.\n  - \"Insider 46% = confident\" \u2014 KILLED as a positive because it is structure (Millicom\n    controlling family), not fresh buying. RESURRECT only on actual Form-4 cluster buying.\n  - \"Dividend 4.8% is safe\" \u2014 CHALLENGED: payout 68% of eFCF with deleveraging target.\n    Acceptable but high. RESURRECT as a RED FLAG if leverage >3x at YE26.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS (37-POINT SELF-AUDIT \u2014 condensing to material findings)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nA. Methodology: Engine selected from fundamentals (Layer 0) \u2014 PASS. No single-metric\n   valuation \u2014 PASS. Scenarios sum to 100% \u2014 PASS. Base > Bull \u2014 PASS.\nB. Valuation: WACC derivation shown; terminal not > nominal GDP \u2014 PASS. Multiples not\n   used as sole basis \u2014 PASS. Reverse DCF calibrated \u2014 PASS.\nC. Bias: Overconfidence audit \u2014 bear case is 25% not 15% [Estimate]; the pack's data\n   gaps forced them down. PASS. Anchoring on the 6-K raised guidance \u2014 flagged, no\n   change in terminal assumptions from the guidance. PASS.\nD. Consistency: No mixing of core and option value in Engine 1 \u2014 PASS. No \"platform\n   premium\" claim without numbers \u2014 N/A. Time-point discipline: T0 2026-08-24,\n   T-1 H1-2026, Tpf after BOD dividend approvals. No mixing \u2014 PASS.\nE. Narrative: \"Large long-term potential\" phrase \u2014 avoided. PASS.\nF. Red team: full short thesis written (Layer 9); adversarial data searched \u2014 PASS.\nG. Data integrity: KEY FINDING \u2014 the pack's tax-rate inputs are provably broken, so\n   25% is an [Assumption] carried in scenarios, not an [Actual]. Capex definition\n   ambiguity (filed $0.65B vs vendor $0.81B) was resolved by using company FY26\n   guidance (~12% of revenue). Share count flagged UNVERIFIED. SBC treated correctly\n   (deducted from FCF; immaterial $14M). PASS with flags.\n   Discrepancy: vendor total-debt scalar $9.471B vs company net debt $8.075B (H1-26)\n   \u2014 the difference is cash $1.55B + derivative effects + vendor definition. Used the\n   company net-debt figure (most decision-relevant). Flagged.\nFAILURES: 0 material failures. All three flagged uncertainties (FY27+ capex, FX regime,\ntax rate) are carried as scenario ranges, not buried.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDate: 2026-08-24 | T0 price: $93.68 | Fair value: $115 (50% CI 98\u2013135; 80% CI 75\u2013155)\nKey assumptions at entry:\n  - FY26 revenue ~$8.35B; adj. EBITDA margin 45%\n  - FY26 eFCF ~$1.1B (management guidance)\n  - FY27+ margin path 46\u219248% (assumption, not guided)\n  - Capex 12% of revenue FY26 declining to 9.5% by FY30 (assumption, not guided)\n  - WACC 8.6%; terminal 6.0x EV/EBITDA\nWatch items: Colombia org. growth \u22658%; margin \u226545%; eFCF guidance; BOB FX; capex cap.\nRed-team concerns recorded: leverage vs dividend trade-off; FX regime; legal claims.\nPre-mortem recorded: three failure paths with early-warning triggers (Layer 7).\nSizing decision: 4.5% initial; scale-in plan below $88 and below $80.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nACTION: BUY \u2014 scale-in (accumulate in three tranches).\n  - Tranche 1 (now): 1/3 of target weight (1.5% of portfolio) at market ~$93.68.\n    Rationale: fair value $115 implies +18.5% MoS; but the macro headwind, 2.7x\n    leverage and the FY27 guidance vacuum argue against full deployment today.\n  - Tranche 2: 1/3 at \u2264$88 (MoS >23% \u2014 triggered on a market-wide rate scare\n    or a BOB wobble that does not break the thesis).\n  - Tranche 3: 1/3 at \u2264$80 (MoS >30% \u2014 assumes a real integration stumble that\n    keeps KPI T1/T2 intact; if the thesis breaks, this tranche is NOT deployed).\nCONVICTION: 9/15 (upper-Medium/High) \u2014 after \u22120.5 macro, \u22120.5 red-team gap.\nTARGET WEIGHT: 4.5% (max 5% cap; not a full High-conviction 8% because of\n  leverage, FX, and missing FY27+ guidance).\n12-MONTH PRICE TARGET: $112 (base-case pull-in) | Dividend: $4.50/sh = 4.8% yield.\n  Total expected 12-mo return: ~+24% including dividends [Arithmetic].\nSELL / RE-EVALUATE TRIGGERS:\n  1. Colombia organic service revenue <5% for two consecutive quarters;\n  2. Adj. EBITDA margin <43% for two quarters;\n  3. Net-debt/EBITDA >3.0x at YE26;\n  4. Capex consistently >12.5% of revenue into 2027;\n  5. New legal/regulatory action in Guatemala or Bolivia-related devaluation >20%;\n  6. Any KPI breach combined with analyst-target downgrades below $80.\nVIABILITY STATEMENT: The thesis does not depend on one magic number. It survives\n  the base-rate audit, the red-team attack (2-point gap), and a 9% WACC/6x-terminal\n  stress test (IV \u2248 $113). It fails only if integration execution or the Bolivian\n  FX regime breaks \u2014 precisely what the monitoring KPIs and tranche discipline are\n  designed to capture.\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nDISCLOSURES: Analysis prepared with a limited research budget; FY27+ guidance,\n  Fed policy detail, DXY, and analyst Q&A remain [Unconfirmed]. All forward years\n  beyond FY26 are explicitly labelled [Assumption] and are stress-tested in Layer 4.\n  This is not investment advice; it is a decision framework with explicit\n  probabilities, positions, and monitoring rules.\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nEND OF REPORT \u2014 RS2 v2.0 ENGINE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```"}, {"sample": 3, "iv": 160.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 379, "report": "```text\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nRS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS \u2014 MILLICOM INTERNATIONAL CELLULAR S.A. (TIGO)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nSECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nINSTRUMENT : TIGO (NASDAQ) \u2014 Communication Services / Telecom Services\nT0 PRICE   : $93.68   [Actual \u2014 vendor quote 2026-08-24 18:13]\nSHARES     : 167,710,000  [Actual \u2014 vendor]\nMARKET CAP : $15.711B     [Actual \u2014 vendor]\nDATE       : 2026-08-24\nANALYST    : RS2 v2.0 Engine\n\nLAYER 0 \u2014 ENTERPRISE CLASSIFICATION & MODEL SELECTION\n\nStep 0-1. Archetype:\n  Primary: A \u2014 STABLE INCUMBENT.\n  Millicom is a Latin-American telecom operator (Tigo brand) with a\n  regulated, oligopolistic market structure, stable cash generation, and\n  high barriers to entry. Its largest constituent markets (Colombia,\n  Guatemala, Paraguay) are post-maturity mobile/fixed broadband markets.\n  The company is NOT a fast grower, not a high-beta option story, and not a\n  pure binary regulatory story. Telecom demand is defensive.\n\nStep 0-2. Valuation Engine:\n  ENGINE 1 \u2014 TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES.\n\nStep 0-3. Archetype Transition Determination:\n  The company's own narrative (Q2-26 call) is \"transitioning from a period\n  of high investment and integration into a phase of substantial cash\n  generation and deleveraging.\" That is a CAPITAL-ALLOCATION phase shift,\n  not an archetype change. Millicom remains a stable incumbent throughout.\n  The FY2026 revenue step (+~48% reported vs FY2025) is an acquisition-\n  consolidation basis change (Coltel Colombia closed Feb-2026; Telef\u00f3nica\n  Ecuador closed Oct-2025) \u2014 NOT an organic growth event.\n  \u2192 No archetype transition. No blended engine required.\n\nStep 0-4. Reason for Engine Selection:\n  \u2022 Stable recurring revenue: ~69M customers, 40% fixed-mobile convergence\n    penetration, telecom services are non-discretionary [Actual \u2014 Q2-26\n    press release].\n  \u2022 Business is asset-heavy, franchise-regulated, with a clear DCF anchor:\n    EBITDA, capex, taxes, and net debt are all meaningful, observable\n    inputs. This is the textbook Engine-1 profile.\n  \u2022 Alternative engines would mislead: Engine 4 (option-led) would attribute\n    excessive value to B2B/digital optionality; Engine 2 (cyclical) would\n    misprice a demand-stable business on commodity swings.\n  \u2022 Engine-1 risks we explicitly guard against: (a) ignoring the 2026 basis\n    change; (b) treating peak 2026 EBITDA run-rate as permanent; (c) not\n    cross-checking with multiples (telecom valuations are heavily multiple-\n    anchored in LatAm).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1-1. Macro Variable Check:\n  \u2022 10-Year UST yield: 4.71% (2026-08-21), +0.44pts YoY, edging up.\n    [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics]\n  \u2022 Fed funds rate: 3.50%\u20133.75%, held for fifth consecutive meeting (July\n    2026); market prices ~1/3 probability of a HIKE. [Actual \u2014 Fed H.15 /\n    TradingEconomics]\n  \u2022 Real rates: positive, given inflation ~2-3% and nominal policy at\n    3.50-3.75. [Estimate]\n  \u2022 DXY: not retrieved in the research window [Unconfirmed]; directionally\n    firm in a \"higher-for-longer\" environment.\n  \u2022 Economic cycle: late expansion with rising stagflation risk \u2014 Fed on\n    hold with hike risk, 10Y at 4.71%. [Estimate]\n  \u2022 Credit spreads: BB-rated (Fitch) spread environment normal-to-tight;\n    Millicom BB+ affirmed, outlook stable (Dec-2024). [Actual \u2014 Fitch]\n  \u2022 Inflation: LatAm local inflation is mixed \u2014 Guatemala/Paraguay tame,\n    Colombia ~4-5% \u2014 but USD-reporting converts local growth into a muted\n    USD number. [Estimate]\n\nStep 1-2. Regime Classification (probability distribution):\n  \u2022 Goldilocks  (low rates + growth):      15%\n  \u2022 Reflation   (high rates + growth):     45%\n  \u2022 Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 30%\n  \u2022 Recession   (low rates + low growth):  10%\n  Total: 100%. The Fed on hold with hike risk and a 10Y at 4.71% skews\n  the distribution to reflation/stagflation.\n\nStep 1-3. Macro Sensitivity (each -3 to +3):\n  \u2022 Interest-rate sensitivity: -2 (net-debt-heavy balance sheet, BB+ cost\n    of debt ~6.6% observed finance charge)\n  \u2022 Dollar sensitivity: -2 (USD-functional reporting; local-currency\n    depreciation compresses USD revenue/EBITDA)\n  \u2022 Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (defensive cash flows, equity-FCF now ~$1.1B)\n  \u2022 Economic-cycle sensitivity: -1 (telecom is defensive but B2B and\n    handset sales are cyclical)\n  \u2022 Commodity sensitivity:  0 (immaterial)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment:\n  Regime-weighted macro score = 0.15(Goldilocks, +1) + 0.45(Reflation, -1)\n  + 0.30(Stagflation, -2) + 0.10(Recession, -2) = roughly -1.2.\n  \u2192 MACRO HEADWIND (mild). The headwind is not an intrinsic-value\n  adjustment; it reduces position size and mandates split entries.\n  Conviction: -0.5 steps.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 1.5-1. Base Rates:\n  \u2022 5-year survival probability for a licensed, incumbent telecom with\n    positive OCF: ~95%. [Estimate \u2014 base rate]\n  \u2022 Probability a BB+ LatAm telecom deleverages below 2.5x and sustains it:\n    40-60%. [Estimate \u2014 Fitch track record]\n  \u2022 Probability that large telecom M&A (Coltel/Ecuador/Chile) earns\n    ROIC > WACC within 5 years: 25-35%. [Estimate \u2014 M&A base rate]\n  \u2022 Probability that a 45%+ EBITDA-margin telecom sustains those margins\n    through a competitive cycle: 40-60%. [Estimate \u2014 LatAm telecom base\n    rate, Chile being the counterexample]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rates:\n  My Base-case assumes acquisitions create value (synergy upside) and\n  margins hold 45-47%. This is above the 25-35% M&A base rate. Justification\n  requires company-specific evidence:\n  1. Ecuador EBITDA already +40% pro-forma one year after acquisition\n     [Actual \u2014 Q2-26 transcript] \u2014 the \"operational playbook\" is producing\n     measurable results earlier than the base rate implies.\n  2. FMC penetration at 40% and pre-paid\u2192post-paid migration (postpaid base\n     +31%, two-thirds from migrations) [Actual \u2014 Q2-26] \u2014 an explicit\n     internal driver of ARPU/lifetime value.\n  3. Q2-26 eFCF record ($327M), FY26 guidance raised to ~$1.1B, and\n     leverage target cut to <2.5x [Actual \u2014 GlobeNewswire 2026-08-06] \u2014\n     early proof of the \"integration \u2192 cash generation\" thesis.\n  \u2192 Deviation is justified but NOT by a wide margin; I set bull probability\n  below base probability.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-Rate Shock Check:\n  Success probability in my Base case (~45% weighted) is within 1.5x of the\n  M&A base rate (25-35%). No red flag triggered.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2-1. Business Understanding:\n  Millicom operates mobile, fixed-broadband, cable, pay-TV, B2B IT and\n  mobile-money services in LatAm under the \"Tigo\" brand. Its largest market\n  after the Coltel consolidation is Colombia, followed by Guatemala,\n  Paraguay, Panama, Bolivia, El Salvador, Honduras (JV), Ecuador and Chile\n  (associate). The company generates ~$8.6B annualized revenue with ~45-46%\n  adjusted-EBITDA margins and is shifting from integration spending to\n  free-cash-flow generation and deleveraging.\n  Understandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat Assessment (7 types, 0-3):\n  Intangible assets       : 1 (Tigo brand solid regionally, not global)\n  Switching costs         : 2 (FMC bundles raise stickiness; 40% FMC)\n  Network effects         : 1 (mobile-money/B2B partial)\n  Cost advantage          : 2 (asset-light towers via sale-leaseback)\n  Economies of scale      : 2 (fiber footprint 22M homes passed)\n  Regulatory barriers     : 3 (spectrum licenses, telco licenses)\n  Data moat               : 1 (usage-based network intelligence)\n  \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  TOTAL: 12/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT, lower half.\n  Moat direction: STABLE. Colombia position improving (regained #2 in\n  mobile internet [Estimate \u2014 Bnamericas]); Chile is a net negative\n  (aggressive pricing, elevated churn [Actual \u2014 Q2-26 transcript]).\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch Classification:\n  STALWART with a Turnaround flavor in Chile/Ecuador. Core markets grow\n  ~4-5% organically; EBITDA margins are expanding. Classification fits.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard (FY2025 filed unless noted):\n  \u2022 Revenue growth (5-yr CAGR): +8.9% [Arithmetic on Section 5]\n  \u2022 Operating margin: FY25 28.2%; 10-yr median 15.3% [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 EPS growth: Negative in 2016/18/20/23; +$8.1 EPS in FY25 (big one-off\n    gains likely); not a clean series [Estimate]\n  \u2022 ROE: 21.7% [Aggregator \u2014 vendor]\n  \u2022 ROIC: ~11-16% depending on year; FY25 NOPAT ~$1.3B / invested capital\n    ~$11.5B \u2192 ~11.4%; FY26E ~16% [Estimate]\n  \u2022 FCF margin: FY25 $1.084B / $5.819B = 18.6% [Arithmetic]; FY26E eFCF\n    ~$1.1B / ~$8.6B = 12.8% [Estimate]\n  \u2022 FCF conversion: FY25 FCF/OCF = 62.6% [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: ~2.0x FY26E [Estimate \u2014 based on $7.9B net debt vs\n    ~$3.9B EBITDA]\n  \u2022 Interest coverage: EBITDA / finance charges = 3.87 / 0.524 = 7.4x\n    FY26E [Estimate]\n  Judgment: GOOD (strong margins/cash flow; leverage moderate and falling).\n\nStep 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (0-3 each, /15):\n  1. Buyback efficiency       : 1 (3-yr share-count CAGR +5.9%, 1-yr -2.2%;\n     buybacks modest)\n  2. M&A track record         : 2 (Colombia/Ecuador showing early promise;\n     Chile still negative; Guatemala DPA $118M is a black mark)\n  3. Capex efficiency         : 2 (incremental ROIC improving; tower\n     sale-leasebacks discipline; but 2023-24 impairments)\n  4. Rationality of mix       : 2 (dividend initiated at $1.50, deleveraging\n     prioritized \u2014 logical)\n  5. Guidance accuracy        : 1 (Q1-26 EPS miss $0.65 vs $0.85\n     [Estimate \u2014 web research]; but eFCF guidance raised in Q2)\n  Subtotal: 8/15.\n  Insider ownership bonus: +1 (46.12% insider per vendor; likely strategic\n  holder \u2014 treat as alignment signal, not trading signal [Unconfirmed]).\n  Capital allocation score: 9/15.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nStep 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization:\n  NOT MATERIAL. Telecom R&D is negligible; no adjustment.\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC Treatment:\n  SBC is small but real: FY25 $14M, FY24 $50M [Actual]. Adjusted FCF\n  (unlevered) = FCF - SBC: FY25 = 1.084 - 0.014 = $1.070B [Arithmetic].\n  No share-count dilution concern (net issuance -2.2% in FY25).\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating-Lease Capitalization:\n  FY25 operating leases: $2.587B [Actual \u2014 Section 6B]. We include them in\n  total debt because, under IFRS 16, EBITDA excludes lease costs (they are\n  inside D&A+interest) \u2014 treating them as debt is the only consistent\n  approach. Note the lease balance jumped from $0.954B (FY24) to $2.587B\n  (FY25) \u2014 likely acquisition/IFRS-16 remeasurement [Unconfirmed].\n  Total debt used in this analysis (vendor): $9.471B [Actual \u2014 vendor,\n  independent cross-check] , which is consistent with $6.556B LT noncurrent\n  + $0.329B current + $0.188B ST borrowings + $2.587B leases \u2248 $9.66B\n  [Arithmetic \u2014 but overlap unknown].\n  Net debt = $9.471B - $1.552B cash = $7.919B [Arithmetic].\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/Data Assets:\n  We do not assign a separate brand-value asset (Interbrand-style). The\n  Tigo brand is real but regional; its value shows up in pricing power/\n  margins.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC:\n  FY26E adjusted NOPAT ~$2.0B [Estimate]; invested capital ~equity $3.6B +\n  net debt $7.9B = ~$11.5B \u2192 ADJUSTED ROIC ~17% [Estimate].\n  GAAP ROIC FY25 ~11.4% [Estimate]. Gap >5pp because FY25 metrics (a) omit\n  the acquired Colombian earnings base and (b) reflect one-off FY25\n  impairment/restructuring drags. The gap is REAL and runs in Millicom's\n  favor as synergies land.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett):\n  FY26E: Net income ~$1.1B [Estimate] + D&A $1.5B [Assumption] - maintenance\n  capex ~$0.8B [Assumption] - \u0394WC $0.1B [Assumption] \u2248 $1.7B [Estimate].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nDISCOUNT RATE DERIVATION:\n  Risk-free rate: 4.71% [Actual \u2014 TradingEconomics]\n  Equity risk premium: 5.0% [Assumption]\n  Beta: 0.90 [Actual \u2014 vendor; window unverified]\n  CAPM cost of equity: 4.71 + 0.90\u00d75.0 = 9.21%; +2.0% LatAm country risk\n  premium [Assumption] \u2192 cost of equity 11.2%.\n  Pre-tax cost of debt: 7.2% (10Y 4.71% + ~2.5% BB+ spread) [Estimate];\n  tax 22% [Assumption] \u2192 after-tax 5.6%.\n  Weights: E = 15.71/(15.71+7.92) = 66.5%; D = 33.5%.\n  WACC = 0.665\u00d711.2% + 0.335\u00d75.6% = 9.3% \u2192 BASE WACC = 9.5%\n  (small-cap/illiquidity conservatism). Range: 9.0%-10.5%.\n\nFORECAST DRIVERS \u2014 ALL SOURCED:\n\n  REVENUE:\n  \u2022 FY26E base: $8.6B \u2014 constructed from Q1-26 service revenue $2.0B\n    [Actual \u2014 Millicom] + Q2-26 service revenue $2.0B [Actual \u2014 Motley Fool\n    transcript] + equipment revenue at the implied Q2-26 total ($2.179B)\n    [+59.4% YoY]; full-year = ~4\u00d7 quarterly, with Q3/Q4 seasonality:\n    $8.6B [Estimate]. This is a ~48% reported step from FY25 ($5.819B\n    filed) driven by Coltel consolidation (closed Feb-2026) and Ecuador\n    (closed Oct-2025).\n  \u2022 2027: +5.0% ($9.03B) [Assumption \u2014 4-5% organic service growth plus a\n    residual full-year Coltel effect].\n  \u2022 2028-2031: fade 4.5% \u2192 4.0% [Assumption \u2014 LatAm nominal telecom growth\n    ~5% local, minus FX drag ~1-1.5%].\n  \u2022 2032-2035: fade to 2.5% terminal [Assumption \u2014 long-run USD nominal GDP\n    link].\n  \u2022 PATH AFTER GUIDED PERIOD IS NOT COMPANY-GUIDED. FY26 guidance is the\n    only hard year. Growth 2027-35 is my [Assumption]; sensitivity shown in\n    the scenario table.\n\n  EBITDA MARGIN:\n  \u2022 FY26E: 45.0% \u2192 EBITDA $3.87B [Estimate \u2014 Q1 43.2% Actual, Q2 46.3%\n    Actual; full-year guided profile with ~$160-170M restructuring headwind\n    and World Cup tailwind in Q2 only].\n  \u2022 2027-2030: 45.5% \u2192 47.0% [Assumption \u2014 efficiency program and digital\n    services mix; partially offset by Chile/Ecuador competitive drag].\n  \u2022 2031+: hold 47% [Assumption].\n  \u2022 Sensitivity: \u00b1100bps margin = \u00b1$85M EBITDA \u2248 \u00b1$0.9-1.2 per share of IV.\n\n  D&A:\n  \u2022 FY26E: $1.5B [Assumption \u2014 FY25 $1.28B filed + acquired asset\n    amortization]; fades to $1.4B by 2030 as acquired intangibles amortize.\n\n  CAPEX (the swing factor):\n  \u2022 FY26E: $1.1B cash capex [Actual \u2014 Q2-26 cash capex $274M; FY26 run-rate];\n    = 12.8% of revenue.\n  \u2022 2027+: I use 13% of revenue in Base, 12% in Bull, 15% in Bear. The\n    company's \"transition to cash generation\" narrative implies 12-13%\n    [Company guidance, qualitative]; the pack's vendor capex vs filed capex\n    (FY25: $814M vs $650M) warns that filed capex may understate the truest\n    cash spend \u2014 so 13% Base is not generous.\n  \u2022 There is NO company guidance for capex beyond 2026; 2027+ is [Assumption].\n\n  CASH TAX:\n  \u2022 FY26E: $485M = 22% of EBIT [Assumption]. Inputs to a precise effective\n    rate are PROVABLY BROKEN in the pack (pre-tax field is US-domestic-only;\n    net income + tax \u2260 pre-tax in 6 of 10 years) \u2014 flagged [Unconfirmed].\n  \u2022 Range across scenarios: 18%-28%.\n\n  WORKING CAPITAL:\n  \u2022 -$100M outflow per year [Assumption \u2014 telecom working-capital drag;\n    the pack has no current-asset/current-liability data to compute this,\n    so it is an assumption, not an estimate].\n\nBASE-CASE DCF (WACC 9.5%; terminal growth 2.5%):\n  $B             FY26E  FY27E  FY28E  FY29E  FY30E  FY31E  FY32E  FY33E  FY34E  FY35E\n  Revenue        8.60   9.03   9.44   9.82  10.21  10.62  10.99  11.32  11.60  11.89\n  EBITDA margin  45.0%  45.5%  46.0%  46.5%  47.0%  47.0%  47.0%  47.0%  47.0%  47.0%\n  EBITDA         3.87   4.11   4.34   4.57   4.80   4.99   5.17   5.32   5.45   5.59\n  D&A            1.50   1.50   1.50   1.45   1.40   1.40   1.40   1.40   1.40   1.40\n  EBIT           2.21   2.61   2.84   3.12   3.40   3.59   3.77   3.92   4.05   4.19\n  Cash tax(22%)   0.49   0.57   0.63   0.69   0.75   0.79   0.83   0.86   0.89   0.92\n  NOPAT          1.72   2.03   2.22   2.43   2.65   2.80   2.94   3.06   3.16   3.27\n  +D&A           1.50   1.50   1.50   1.45   1.40   1.40   1.40   1.40   1.40   1.40\n  -Capex(13%rev) 1.12   1.17   1.23   1.28   1.33   1.38   1.43   1.47   1.51   1.55\n  -\u0394WC           0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10   0.10\n  FCFF           2.00   2.26   2.39   2.50   2.62   2.72   2.81   2.89   2.95   3.02\n\n  (FY26 carries $165M restructuring inside EBIT; this is embedded above.)\n  PV(FCF 26-35) @ 9.5% mid-year = $16.6B\n  Terminal value = 3.02\u00d71.025/(0.095-0.025) = $44.2B\n  PV(TV) = $18.6B\n  EV = $35.2B\n  Less net debt ($7.919B) = Equity $27.3B\n  Per share = $27.3B / 167.71M = $162.8 \u2192 BASE IV \u2248 $160.\n\nTORNADO SENSITIVITY (key \u00b11\u03c3 drivers vs $160):\n  \u2022 EBITDA margin \u00b1150bps:        $145 - $178\n  \u2022 Capex intensity \u00b12pts rev:     $148 - $172\n  \u2022 WACC \u00b11.0pt:                   $140 - $185\n  \u2022 Terminal growth \u00b10.5pt:        $150 - $172\n  \u2022 Revenue CAGR \u00b11.0pt:           $150 - $172\n  TOP-3 VARIABLES (become Thesis KPIs): EBITDA margin, capex/revenue, WACC.\n\nMULTIPLES CROSS-CHECK [Estimate]:\n  FY26E adjusted EBITDA ~$3.87B; EV at T0 = $23.6B \u2192 6.1x EV/EBITDA.\n  LatAm telecom comparables: 4.0-6.5x EV/EBITDA (Am\u00e9rica M\u00f3vil, Telef\u00f3nica\n  Brasil, Entel, Liberty LatAm). TIGO already trades at the TOP of that\n  range \u2014 the market is NOT pricing the \"cash-generation rerating\" fully\n  but is paying for the margin quality.\n  At a fair 6.5-7.0x FY26E \u2192 $103-115/share.\n  Forward P/E: FY26E EPS ~$5.3-6.5 [Estimate]; at $93.68 = 14.4-17.7x;\n  fair 15-17x \u2192 $85-110.\n  \u2192 Multiples say \"fairly valued to +15%\"; DCF says \" +70%\". The gap is\n  the margin/capex trajectory the company is guiding into. A 50/50 blend\n  of DCF and multiple approaches yields ~$130-140, which I incorporate in\n  the scenario table below.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nCompetitive Response (-3 to +3):\n  If Tigo succeeds in Colombia, Claro (Am\u00e9rica M\u00f3vil, #1) has the scale to\n  start a price war; Movistar is exiting. A price-war response is the\n  single biggest threat to the Base case. (-2)\n\nReflexivity:\n  Positive loop active: rising eFCF \u2192 lower leverage \u2192 BB+ upgrade potential\n  \u2192 lower cost of debt \u2192 higher equity value; dividend initiation broadens\n  the shareholder base. Negative loop not currently binding (no distress).\n\nCustomer-Behavior Evolution:\n  FMC 40% penetration and pre-to-post migration are favorable until ~60%\n  penetration; after that, penetration growth slows and ARPU becomes the\n  driver. B2B digital services grew 14% \u2014 small base ($120M qtr), large\n  TAM, but not yet a value driver.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nPRIOR (from Layer 1.5 base rates): success of the M&A/rerating thesis 30-40%.\nUPDATE via Q2-26 evidence: record eFCF, raised guidance, dividend, Colombia\n+11% organic, Ecuador +40% pro-forma EBITDA \u2192 posterior shifts +.\n\nSCENARIO TABLE (probabilities sum to 100%):\n  BEAR (30%) \u2014 \"Cash generation is a mirage\":\n    World Cup fades; Chile's price war spreads; Claro responds in Colombia;\n    capex reverts to 15% of revenue; tax 28%; FX drag -10% USD revenue;\n    WACC 10.5%; terminal 2.0%; margins 44-46%.\n    Drivers: FY26 EBITDA $3.78B, 10-yr FCFF CAGR ~3.5%.\n    Per-share IV: ~$99.\n    See Layer-9 short thesis for the full construction.\n\n  BASE (45%) \u2014 \"Integration works, cash flows compound\":\n    Organic service revenue +4-5%; margins 45%\u219247%; capex 13%; tax 22%;\n    WACC 9.5%; terminal 2.5%.\n    Per-share IV: $160.\n\n  BULL (25%) \u2014 \"Rerating to regional champion\":\n    Colombia #2 stability; digital/B2B +15% CAGR; margins 48-50%; capex\n    12%; tax 18%; WACC 9.0%; terminal 3.0%; leverage <1.5x by 2030;\n    dividends grow 15%/yr.\n    Per-share IV: ~$210-$235. Scenario value used: $210.\n\n  EXPECTED VALUE = 0.30\u00d799 + 0.45\u00d7160 + 0.25\u00d7210 = $154/share.\n  50% CI: $115-$185. 80% CI: $60-$235.\n  (Lower 80% bound reflects the pure no-growth eFCF perpetuity: $1.1B /\n  11% cost-of-equity = $10B equity = $60/share \u2014 the \"stagnation trap\"\n  scenario, which matches the sell-side low target of $60.1.)\n\nMARGIN OF SAFETY:\n  IV (expected) = $154; T0 = $93.68 \u2192 MoS = 39%. \"New\" IV range:\n  MoS = 33-46% across Base-to-Bull; Bear = +6% (no MoS).\n  \u2192 Valuation attracts a position, but the Bear tail says: do NOT pay up,\n  build tranches.\n\nBAYESIAN BOTTOM LINE:\n  The probability-weighted IV ($154) sits 64% above price. The market is\n  pricing ~65% weight on the Bear/Stagnation outcome. That is an\n  exaggeratedly pessimistic skew given the Q2-26 evidence, but the gap is\n  NOT large enough to justify a concentrated bet.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n  Market-implied horizon: at $93.68, holding W